[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: One node, one topic: keep nodes small and let count carry complexity

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[WORK]
UID: WORK-ONE-NODE-ONE-TOPIC
TITLE: One node, one topic: keep nodes small and let count carry complexity
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Nodes are small: one node, one topic. The plan tree should carry one node per
wanted thing, so a plan-view item has a tree-view child and a kanban child,
and every spec item beneath them is its own node. Node count should reflect
the amount of specificity or complexity, not the length of any one node. No
hard limit, but a decision node is one decision, a mechanism is one mechanic,
a use case is one path, and every other type follows the same line. Observed
this session as the pattern to unpack at grooming: several nodes grew without
bound by appending verbatim paragraphs to NOTES (WORK-ANNOTATION-NODES,
WORK-CODE-LINKAGE-EXTRACTORS, WORK-GRAMMAR-TAB-GROUPS). Companion to
MECH-ONE-NODE-PER-FILE, which fixes the file unit; this fixes the topic unit.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Operator, 2026-09-02, verbatim: "dont fix now just file another node or update
a node, nodes are meant to be small. one node one topic. i was expecting like
plan-view node, tree node, kanban node under plan-view, every spec item under
each is defining what i want is a single node. just skimmed through and I see
some mega nodes with unbound growth. node count should reflect the amount of
specificity or complexity based on count. asking just to file in the shared
understanding plan to unpack more details on this. i dont want to make a hard
limit but a decision node is one decision, same with mechanic, use case, and
pretty much every node along the same lines."
